{
  "id": "binary-tree-level-order-traversal-ii",
  "questionId": "binary-tree-level-order-traversal-ii",
  "slug": "binary-tree-level-order-traversal-ii",
  "title": "Binary Tree Level Order Traversal II",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Tree bfs",
  "patterns": [
    "tree bfs",
    "queue"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "this is exactly level order traversal - only the final ordering of the rows changes, not how you discover them",
    "run the standard BFS, processing one level at a time and building each level's list left to right, same as always",
    "instead of appending each finished level to the end of your result, either insert it at the front, or just append normally and reverse the whole result once at the very end",
    "the values within a level still read left to right - only the order of the rows themselves is flipped"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "reversing the values within each level instead of reversing the order of the levels themselves",
    "inserting at the front of a plain array/list repeatedly, which is correct but can be accidentally O(n^2) in some languages if not using an appropriate data structure - appending then reversing once avoids that",
    "forgetting to reverse at all and returning a normal top-down traversal"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A binary tree is given as a plain integer array named `tree`, using a complete-binary-tree (heap) index layout:\n\n- the root sits at index 0\n- a node at index `i` has its left child at index `2*i+1` and right child at index `2*i+2`\n- a child index only counts as real if it's within the array bounds **and** its value isn't the sentinel `-101`, which means \"no node here\"\n\nFor example, `[1, 2, 3, -101, 4]` encodes this tree:\n\n```\n      1\n     / \\\n    2   3\n     \\\n      4\n```\n\nNode 2 has no left child but does have a right child, 4.\n\n**Group the tree's node values level by level, left to right within each level, but list the levels themselves from the deepest level first up to the root's level last (bottom-up instead of top-down).**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= length of tree <= 2000",
      "every real node value is in the range -100 to 100",
      "the sentinel value -101 marks a missing node and never appears as a real value",
      "the result has one row per level, ordered from deepest level to root level"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node tree",
      "a two-level tree, where only the order of two rows needs to be swapped",
      "a tree that is a chain, so every level has exactly one value"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [3, 9, 20, -101, -101, 15, 7]",
        "output": "[[15, 7], [9, 20], [3]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[15, 7], [9, 20], [3]]",
        "explanation": "the deepest level [15, 7] comes first, then [9, 20], then the root level [3] last"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]",
        "output": "[[4, 5, 6, 7], [2, 3], [1]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[4, 5, 6, 7], [2, 3], [1]]",
        "explanation": "the deepest level of a perfectly complete 3-level tree comes first"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "tree = [1]",
        "output": "[[1]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[1]]",
        "explanation": "a single level is trivially both first and last"
      }
    ],
